WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.070
- [Announcer] This Great Big
Story was made possible by

00:00:02.070 --> 00:00:06.573
Wells Fargo, established
1852, re-established 2018.

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:09.760
- [Narrator] This is a
story about a dinner.

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:11.880
But not just any dinner. This is a dinner

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:14.660
that involves this
kitchen, with these cooks.

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:17.020
And this garden, with these gardeners.

00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:18.660
It's a center that's helping a community

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:20.463
fight homelessness in a big way.

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:26.830
San Francisco has a large
population of homeless people.

00:00:26.830 --> 00:00:29.120
Just last year, over 4,000 homeless people

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:32.273
were reported unsheltered -
and over 6,000 unemployed.

00:00:33.170 --> 00:00:34.670
Enter Farming Hope.

00:00:34.670 --> 00:00:36.650
A non-profit created to help those in need

00:00:36.650 --> 00:00:39.000
by training them in urban
farming, and cooking,

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:42.040
in the hopes of helping them
find full-time employment.

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:44.010
Joining us from the
Farming Hope initiative

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:48.060
is Jamie, Kevin, and Asha.

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:50.270
- We just wanted to be the organizers

00:00:50.270 --> 00:00:52.810
to piece this knowledge
together of how do we grow food

00:00:52.810 --> 00:00:55.570
and cook it in a way that
opens up our food system,

00:00:55.570 --> 00:00:56.860
that gets people the job,

00:00:56.860 --> 00:00:58.510
and the training that they need and want.

00:00:58.510 --> 00:01:00.380
So what we do is we recruit folks

00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:01.720
who are experiencing homelessness

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:03.410
to join the Farming Hope community

00:01:03.410 --> 00:01:05.640
and put our mission into action.

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:06.680
- [Narrator] But for this to work,

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:08.500
participants have to
learn the true meaning

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:09.413
of farm-to-table.

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:14.250
So the first step is in
the garden with Asha.

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:17.980
- My role is to help facilitate
the employees in the garden.

00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:19.330
So since we just planted this one,

00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:20.870
maybe we won't harvest it.

00:01:20.870 --> 00:01:23.830
But we can harvest from
the rooftop garden.

00:01:23.830 --> 00:01:26.780
We water, we're also growing new plants,

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:28.400
so we're putting seeds in the ground.

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:31.080
We harvest every week for the kitchen.

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:33.660
Kevin uses these in his tacos.

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:37.449
And so being here and encouraging them.

00:01:37.449 --> 00:01:38.720
Beautiful.

00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:41.470
To get back in touch with themselves

00:01:41.470 --> 00:01:43.240
just as much as learning about gardening,

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:44.403
I think is important.

00:01:45.670 --> 00:01:47.050
Then once the produce is grown,

00:01:47.050 --> 00:01:48.900
it's off to the kitchen with Kevin.

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:50.640
- My role in the kitchen is to facilitate

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:52.480
a lot of the hands-on
training that we're doing

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:54.410
with our transitional homeless folks.

00:01:54.410 --> 00:01:58.260
We do a lot of stuff from
knife basics, time management,

00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:01.280
cleanliness, stuff that can
be directly transferable

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:03.793
to other jobs, either
in food or not in food.

00:02:05.870 --> 00:02:07.060
I'm always really inspired

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:08.020
by the people that we're working with,

00:02:08.020 --> 00:02:09.410
'cause they're the ones that

00:02:09.410 --> 00:02:10.740
really dedicate themselves to the work.

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:12.840
They're here; they're
putting in 110 percent,

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:16.471
so I think our folks really
deserve a lot of the applause.

00:02:16.471 --> 00:02:18.320
(chuckles)

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:19.980
- [Narrator] And finally,
the team gets to enjoy

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:21.320
the fruits of their labor.

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:23.670
Each week, they serve up
a meal to both homeless,

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:25.030
and non-homeless people.

00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:27.790
- [Kevin] So for tonight's
menu, we started off with

00:02:27.790 --> 00:02:29.720
a roasted butternut squash taco.

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:32.450
The second taco, that's a stewed zucchini.

00:02:32.450 --> 00:02:35.490
The last taco is a fried rockfish taco.

00:02:35.490 --> 00:02:36.690
And then lastly for desert,

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:39.530
we're serving a vegan chocolate mousse.

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:41.550
- [Narrator] And while for
some eating is the best part,

00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:43.720
for Philip, it's the experience.

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:46.690
- I was homeless for almost six months

00:02:46.690 --> 00:02:48.560
for the very first time in my life.

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:50.160
I was introduced to Farmers Hope.

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:53.103
So I learned a whole lot.
- [Asha] Can you smell it,

00:02:53.103 --> 00:02:54.740
it smells like lemons a little bit.

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:55.573
- Right.

00:02:55.573 --> 00:02:57.440
It kept me there thinking about my life.

00:02:57.440 --> 00:02:59.880
So when I'm there, I have peace.

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:01.883
They gave me a new place to go.

00:03:03.020 --> 00:03:04.940
- I think the most unique
part of Farming Hope

00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:08.740
is gathering people from
distinct walks of life

00:03:08.740 --> 00:03:12.290
to break bread together, and
just be next to each other.

00:03:12.290 --> 00:03:14.860
Not every moment of our
life has to be about

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:17.450
fixing everything, and
it actually has to start

00:03:17.450 --> 00:03:21.020
from that place of empathy, of
awareness, of neighborliness.

00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:23.830
And by involving everybody
at the same level

00:03:23.830 --> 00:03:27.130
on a dining table, it allows
us to open the space up

00:03:27.130 --> 00:03:31.163
to actual dialogue, actual
empathy, actual solutions.

